or.\n\n2.  **(B, 3) through (B, 6)**: These moves break into the empty "West" zone (A and B columns). While valid, playing adjacent to the opponent\'s wall (C-column) often provokes a response on the B-column or A-column (edge), leading to a complex exchange where the opponent might gain access to corners A1 or A8. For example, playing (B, 3) flips C4 and D5 but allows the opponent to play (A, 3) or (B, 4), keeping the initiative.\n\n3.  **(E, 1)**: This is a safe edge move that flips E2 and E3. It consolidates the E-column. However, it creates a block of pieces that might be attacked from the D or F columns later (e.g., F1). It is a decent move but less impactful than H4.\n\n4.  **(H, 4)**: This is an edge move. It flips G4 and F4.\n    *   **Safety**: After playing H4, the opponent cannot immediately capture this piece or play on adjacent edge squares (H3, H5) because they lack the necessary anchors to flip H4.\n    *   **Impact**: It converts the right-side pieces (F4, G4) to your color, reducing the opponent\'s mobility in that area.\n    *   **Strategic Value**: It secures an edge without giving the opponent a corner or a way to wedge in immediately. It forces the opponent to play back into the center (e.g., at G5) or open the B-column themselves.\n\n**Reasoning:**\nThe move **(H, 4)** is the strongest choice. It secures a stable position on the edge and flips two opponent pieces, shifting the balance of power on the right side of the board. Unlike the move (G, 3), which disastrously gives the edge to the opponent, (H, 4) claims the edge for yourself. Unlike the moves on the B-column, it does not open up a new front where the opponent has a solid wall of pieces to leverage against you. It restricts the opponent\'s mobility while building your own stable territory.\n\nEvaluation: H4 is a stable edge move that captures opponent territory and restricts their mobility without immediate risk of recapture or corner exposure.\nReason: Moves like G3 give the opponent t